# localsend

LocalSend, the file sharing app Omarchy v4.0.4 preinstalls, packaged for @DISTRO_NAME@'s repository (docs/6, choice 19). Omarchy ships LocalSend from its own repository and Ubuntu's archive has none; `@DISTRO_ID@-desktop` depends on this package, so the menu's Share entries (clipboard, file, folder) and Files' "Send via LocalSend" have it to call.

The package keeps the upstream name, as the policy says for third-party software we rebuild, with the version `1.18.2-0@DISTRO_ID@1`. It holds LocalSend's own release builds, not a build from source: the app tarball and the CLI tarball from the GitHub release, unpacked under `/usr/lib/localsend` with `/usr/bin/localsend` and `/usr/bin/localsend-cli` as symlinks, the layout of Omarchy's package.

| Path | What |
|---|---|
| `upstream` | the two release tarballs' URLs and SHA-256s (the CLI as the component `cli`), `NO_TOP_DIR=1` because neither tarball has a top-level directory, and `RELEASES=localsend/localsend` for `make upstream-check` |
| `debian/rules` | copies the app's directory and the CLI into place, leaves out `lib/libdartjni.so` (it needs a Java runtime), installs the desktop entry and the release's logos as icons, and strips nothing |
| `debian/localsend.desktop` | the launcher, `Exec=localsend %F` |

A new LocalSend release: `make upstream-check` reports it; update the version in the two URLs and both SHA-256s in `upstream` (GitHub shows each file's SHA-256 on the release page), add a changelog entry, then `make build PKG=packages/localsend`.
